      "AND MOVING IT FOUND A BUG THE MACHINE PAGE HAD BEEN DRAWING SINCE IT WAS WRITTEN. The hot segment was `.head`, and `.head` is a PART in this language — the engraved faceplate header, `display:flex` with 14px of padding and a border under it. `.meter i.on.head` sets a colour and a glow and overrides no size at all, so the one segment in the middle of a 22px track wore the header's padding and blew up to 30 by 26, lit, standing out of its own recess. It has been doing that on MACHINE 07 for as long as that page has had a running job. Nobody looked, because a plotter job on a documentation page is something you start and read the numbers of; the same segment in a modal, two inches from a paragraph you are reading, is impossible to miss. THE PART DID NOT GET WORSE BY MOVING — it got LOOKED AT.",
      "IT IS `.hot` NOW, IN THE STYLESHEET, IN THE KIT AND ON THE MACHINE PAGE, and the rule it broke is written into SYSTEM 13 beside it: a state class on a shared element has to be as specific as the part it might collide with. `.on`, `.done` and `.hot` are clean; `.head` never was, and the giveaway was always there — it is a NOUN, and every other state class in this file is an adjective. A sweep of the rest of the stylesheet for the same shape turns up `.empty`, which is a placeholder card at 38px of padding AND the state of an asset row's thumb and of a pen cap. Both of those pin their own size, so nothing is visibly wrong today; it is the same trap with its teeth in a different place, and it is written down rather than quietly changed under two parts nobody asked about.",
      "THE PROGRESS TRACK MOVED UP OUT OF THE MACHINE, AND IT WAS NEVER A MACHINE PART. `.meter` was written for a plotter job and the argument for it had nothing to do with plotters: a fill creeping 0.3% a minute is indistinguishable from a fill that has stopped, which is the one question actually being asked of a progress bar. Segments quantise it — the lit count is readable across a room and the next one arriving is an EVENT. That is as true of an upload as of a plot, and an upload is where it turned up next. The rules are byte for byte what MACHINE 07 has been running; skew-machine.css now has a note where they were instead of a copy, and its header says eight where it said nine.",
