      "AND `orbitBay.set` WAS NOT SILENT, WHICH EVERY OTHER `.set` IN THIS FILE IS. A `knob`'s exposed setter ends in `onChange` unconditionally — its quiet flag silences the SOUND and nothing else — so pushing a pose into the bay ran the bay's own knob handler three times. That handler LIGHTS an axis and calls the host back, so a host syncing the bay to a pose it already knew about lit all three channels in turn, left the last one glowing for 420ms, and reported three edits nobody made. The fix is the `syncing` flag already in the function, raised around the writes and RESTORED rather than cleared, because `set` can be reached from inside a sync.",
      "IT IS INVISIBLE ON THIS PAGE, WHICH IS WHY IT SHIPPED. The viewport here turns the ball with `turn()` and never syncs it, so no caller on this site can show the fault. An app whose camera is also draggable on its own canvas has one on every pointermove — Portrait-ribbons does — and what it looks like there is the ROLL wire lit while you are dragging yaw.",
      "A FRAME METER ON SYSTEM 12, BECAUSE \u201cis this slow or is it my machine\u201d IS NOT A QUESTION ONE NUMBER CAN ANSWER. It keeps two: IDLE, what this browser does with nothing asked of it, and HELD, what it does while a pointer is down \u2014 sampled into separate windows and flushed whenever the pointer state changes. Idle high and held collapsed is the work; both low is the machine or the browser; and neither is a thing you can tell from watching a single figure move. WORST is there for judder, because an average of 40fps made of even frames feels nothing like the same average made of one 200ms stall, and only the second is a bug. RE-GLAZE is the one cost the page can time from outside, since it is the one the bench calls itself: four re-glazes, the bay and three balls, every knob step.",
      "AND A DRAG IS LOGGED, BECAUSE A NUMBER THAT MOVES IS NOT EVIDENCE. The readouts say what is happening now and there is no way to hand that to anybody. A drag is a closed thing with a start and an end, so it gets a record on release: what was held \u2014 named off the kit\u2019s own wrappers and the caption already in the DOM, so nothing is hard-coded against a control that might get renamed \u2014 how long, how far the hand went, how many events, what each cost, and what the frame rate did while it cost that. Every sample is kept rather than averaged as it goes, because the mean is the figure least likely to be the problem: a handler costing 2ms with one 40ms stall in it averages under four and feels broken, and only the spread shows it, so p50 and MAX are printed beside it. One button puts the session on the clipboard, falling back to printing the array where the clipboard is refused \u2014 which is any page not on https or localhost.",
      "AND A CONTROL CAN HOLD SIXTY AND STILL FEEL HEAVY, WHICH IS WHY THE METER TIMES THE HANDLER TOO. If the work for one pointermove takes most of a frame the frames still arrive on time \u2014 they just each carry a value the hand has already moved on from, and what you feel is LATENCY, not judder. Sixty with a saturated frame and sixty with an idle one are the same number and completely different controls. Every pointer handler in the kit is bound to an ELEMENT \u2014 `cv`, `k`, `knob`, `track`, never document \u2014 so a capture-phase listener on the window runs before all of them and a bubble-phase one runs after all of them, and the gap between the two is the work the control did for that event. EVENT MS is that gap averaged; INPUT % is the same figure against wall clock. Past about seventy there is no headroom left and the control trails the hand however good the frame rate looks.",
      "IT MEASURES THE BROWSER AND NEVER REACHES INSIDE `orbit`, which is the point. Frame-to-frame time is enough \u2014 a paint that overruns blocks the main thread and the next frame is late by exactly what it cost \u2014 so there is no instrumentation to leave behind in a shipped control and nothing to remember to take out. It is doc-only and enforced: `fpsmeter` and `fpscell` are in `build-dist`\u2019s exclude list beside `orbench`, and every selector names one of those two and nothing else. Pair either with `.kval` and the rule stops being doc-only and ships, which is why the readouts are addressed as bare children.",
