      "THE RANGE FADER'S ENDS ARE JAWS NOW, NOT CAPS, AND THE OLD PAIR HAD A FAILURE IT COULD NOT SEE. A cap is 17px wide and centred on its value, so two of them merge into one lump whenever span/(max-min) drops below CAP_W/trackWidth — about nine per cent of the range on a panel row. `MIN_SPAN` was guarding the VALUES at two per cent and the PIXELS at nothing, which is why the caps had been overlapping for a long time before the clamp ever fired: at the floor, on a 190px slot, two 17px caps sat 4px apart. Every attempt to fix that by moving the caps apart trades one unreadable state for another, because a nudged cap no longer stands where its value is.",
      "SO THE SHAPE CARRIES IT INSTEAD OF THE ARITHMETIC. Each end is a GRIP, a NECK and a BLADE — the blade standing at the value, the mass behind it pointing AWAY from the band, start jaw and end jaw facing outward with the lit band between them. The blade is the tallest piece because it is the one that means something: its inner face is where the band stops. The grip is the square cap, outboard, knurled, and it is what a thumb goes on. The neck is the thin section between them, laid down first so the other two paint over its ends — three parts in a row is three parts, but a thin section tucked under two thick ones is one moulding with a waist in it. Because each jaw's mass sits on the far side of its own bar, the two bodies CANNOT overlap however close the values get. At zero span the blades butt together and the grips are still a full jaw apart on either side, both still there to take a thumb. Nothing is nudged, offset or faded to keep them apart; they are apart because of what they are.",
