      "EVERY DRAG IS RELATIVE NOW, WHICH ABSOLUTE COULD AFFORD NOT TO BE. A cap was centred on its value, so jumping the value to the pointer moved nothing; a jaw is grabbed by a triangle sitting up to a jaw-width off its bar, and an absolute drag would yank the end sideways the moment you touched it. A bare-track hit snaps first and then runs relative, which is the same thing as absolute once the value is under the pointer.",
      "NO TRIANGLE, AND TWO OF THEM HAD TO SHIP BEFORE THAT WAS OBVIOUS. The first was the transport glyph literally — a 12x22 arrowhead — and it read as a drawing of a part rather than a part. The second blunted the tip to a short vertical face on the theory that the problem was the knife edge. It was not. A TAPERING SHAPE HAS ITS LEAST MATERIAL EXACTLY WHERE YOU LOOK AT IT HARDEST, so there is nothing there to shade, and no amount of rim, bevel or shadow fixes that — the fix is to stop tapering. The EQ-200's caps are the reference and they are square, solid blocks: bright top rim, black waist, hard shadow underneath, mass everywhere. The jaw is built out of that block, which is the one shape this panel already knew how to make.",
      "AND `clip-path` GOES WITH THE TAPER, WHICH IS HALF THE WIN. Every difficulty on the pointed versions was that one property. It clips POINTER EVENTS, so the part was ungrabbable exactly at the tip, which is exactly where the two ends meet and exactly where a hand needs it. It clips INSET SHADOWS, so the diagonals — the only edges that made the shape anything — were the two edges with no light on them, and the bevel had to be rebuilt as a second layer showing a pixel proud of the first. And it is applied AFTER filters, so a drop-shadow standing in for that bevel was drawn and then trimmed off at the very silhouette it was meant to sit outside. A rounded rectangle needs none of it: the three pieces take the cap's own six inset shadows unmodified, because they ARE the cap's shape.",
      "AND THE ENDS OF THE RANGE WERE UNREACHABLE, which is the bug a relative drag buys if the POSITION is clamped instead of the value. The value is `grabFrom + (vAt - grabV)`, and grabbing a jaw by its grip puts the pointer a jaw-width before the blade, so grabV is less than a. Clamp `t` at 0 and that offset is preserved for ever: push the hand off the left of the track and the best the start end can reach is `a - grabV + min`, short of the floor by exactly however far up the wedge you happened to grab. Measured on a 190px slot it stopped at 0.08, and it stopped somewhere different every time because it depended on where you took hold. The position runs past both stops now and `q` clamps the value, which is where the limit actually lives.",
