      'A FLAT FACET CANNOT HAVE A SOFT GRADIENT ACROSS IT, and that is what was wrong with the flange for four rounds. Its normal is the same at every point, so it reflects the same direction at every point — there is no cosine falloff to have, because nothing about it is turning away from anything. A cosine across the ring was the DOME\'s law applied to a surface that is not a dome, and it read as exactly what it was: soft, and therefore flat. What a flat facet really shows is an area light WITH ITS EDGES ON, so the cosine is clipped into a patch with ends.',
      'A SPHERE IS A MIRROR BALL, AND THAT IS NOT A FIGURE OF SPEECH — the reflected ray is 2(n·v)n − v, its azimuth is the pixel\'s own and its elevation is exactly TWICE. Which is why the first version of the streaks drew a closed OVAL: a band of sky at constant height is a loop on a mirror ball, correctly, and it is not what a lit dome looks like. A light is a light, with an angular size, and the mapping curves it round by itself. The doubling is also why the highlights sit so high — a lamp 76° off the view axis reflects at 38°, which lands at 0.62 of the radius, where a lamp in FRONT would put its highlight in the middle of the face.',
      'THE GLASS CONTAINS THE LIGHT. A dome over the bore is a lid, so the plate\'s light does not arrive on it: no wash on the collar while an axis is driven, no white bottom highlight in the seat or the mouth, and no pool of light under the ball. The feedback moves inboard rather than away — the arc, the live colour on the well lips and the cast light on the ball\'s underside all still say which axis is live. Every one of those is scoped to `:not(.glazed)` rather than removed, so an unglazed ball is byte-for-byte the part it was.',
      'AND THE SOCKET\'S SHADOW MOVES UNDER THE CANVAS WITH IT. `.orbit-mouth` at z-index 2 is right for an open bore — the shadow is in FRONT of a ball sitting down in a hole — and wrong in front of the glass that CAPS the hole. Left on top it did not dim the dome\'s marks, it deleted them: measured, the three inset shadows are about 90% opaque where the dome\'s edge lift sits. The ball does not lose its edge by this, because that darkening was always doubled — `VIG` does the same job per pixel from the inside.',
      'THE GLASS IS GEOMETRY, NOT LIGHTING, and splitting `glaze()` out of `buildGrid` is what makes the bench usable. The dome never turns, so every value is a property of the box exactly like the normals — it was in the paint loop costing 0.8ms of every full pass at `lg` for an answer identical every time. A knob turn is not a resize either: `buildGrid` allocates six arrays and takes two square roots a pixel, none of which changes when the material does. Now a drag reads one number per pixel and a re-glaze is 0.5 / 0.9 / 1.6 ms across the three sizes.',
      'ITS OUTER EDGE IS THE ONLY SILHOUETTE THIS PART DRAWS IN THE OPEN, so it is the only one that ever needed feathering — the ball\'s rim goes under the socket\'s shadow and the cuts are supersampled. It was a hard binary cutoff on the device-pixel grid, which on a circle is a staircase all the way round. Linear coverage over `aa` device pixels, recomputed with the grid because the drag runs at DPR 0.75 and the still at 1.25.',
      'AND THE FLANGE WAS SUBTRACTING LIGHT. With the azimuthal term three times its base, the far side came out below zero — which on the BALL was taken off the colour and darkened it, while in the socket wall the same value clamped to a transparent alpha. One surface, two behaviours, and neither is what a reflection does: light can fail to arrive, it cannot be removed.',
