      'THE ORBIT BALL\'S ARCS TURN WITHOUT LIMIT, AND THE BALL ALREADY DID. Dragging a ring ran out at about half a turn in either direction and then reversed under the hand. There was nothing to tune: `signedAngle` is an atan2 between two unit vectors, so ±180° is not a limit imposed on the answer, it is the entire range the quantity has — and Holroyd\'s sheet only ever answers with z > 0, so a ring seen edge on folded back well short of even that. Three attempts to lift the ceiling by adjusting it did not ship, which is what a wrong quantity does to the people tuning it.',
      'SO A RING IS A WHEEL YOU PUSH, NOT AN AZIMUTH YOU POINT AT. Under a small turn about the ring\'s axis the point under the hand moves at `axis × p`; project that to the screen and it is the direction the hand has to travel to wind the ring forward, and how far the hand actually went along it is the angle. It is a RATE, so nothing is compared to where the drag started and the turns simply keep adding — which is exactly the shape the body roll took at 1.94.0, so both halves of the control now behave the same way.',
      'AND THE THING THAT BOUNDED THE OLD READ IS WHAT MAKES THE NEW ONE GO. Because the sheet always answers with z > 0, the screen tangent under the hand cannot change sign during a drag: monotone by construction, no fold to run into because there is no far side to reach.',
      'THE HAND IS CLAMPED TO THE SILHOUETTE BEFORE IT IS PROJECTED, and without that the wall simply moved out one turn. Outside the ball the sheet flattens and everything far out maps into a tiny neighbourhood of the ring\'s pole, where the tangent points almost straight into the page — measured, a vertical drag on an edge-on ring died at 382° and thirty ball radii of hand bought no more than eight did. Past the rim only the BEARING is information; the magnitude says nothing about which way the ring is being wound. Dropping it takes the same drag to 1719°, linear in how far you pushed.',
      'THE TANGENT IS DIVIDED OUT ONCE, NOT TWICE, and this is the trap. Dividing by |t|² is the least-squares answer and it reads as the better one — the lever arm becomes the pointer\'s own distance from the axis, so pushing near the hub turns further per pixel, which is what a wheel does. It is also the exact derivative of the position model and inherits its blow-up whole: on an edge-on X ring it works out to dθ = -dy/√(1-y²), unbounded sensitivity at the rim, and on a face-on one it is unbounded at the hub. One division is a constant gain and has neither.',
      '`RING_GAIN` IS 1 AND SOMETHING READS IT AT LAST. It arrived at 1.94.0 with the drag it was written for, that drag did not ship, and the constant sat here for two versions describing itself as "a shade under the free roll\'s" — which is π/2, and 0.6 is not a shade under anything. A ring\'s radius is one ball radius and arc = rθ, so a radius of hand travel along the tangent is one radian of ring: arithmetic, not taste, and the same claim the arcball makes. The arrow painted on the ring stays under the pointer instead of lagging it.',
      'FOUR HELPERS AND A VARIABLE WENT WITH THE POSITION MODEL. Neither drag projects any more, so `flatten`, `signedAngle` and the two products they were built from are gone, and `from` with them — both branches measure a delta, so where the grab was is not a quantity either of them has a use for. Its last job, telling the hover handler that a drag was live, is what `held` says. The two comments left contradicting each other at 1.94.0 — one describing this drag, the other conceding the ceiling as permanent — are one comment again, and it now describes the code underneath it.',
      'THE HONEST COST: a rate is not a position, so an arc dragged out and back by a DIFFERENT route does not land where it started. Retraced exactly, it does. That is the trade the body roll already took and argued for, and double-click still turns the whole thing back to zero.',
