      'THE ORIENTATION IS A MATRIX NOW, AND THE THREE ANGLES ARE A READOUT. It was the other way round, and that is where the pole lock came from: every drag step built a matrix out of yaw/pitch/roll, turned it, and decomposed the result straight back — so the angles were the state and the matrix was scratch. Euler angles cannot carry an orientation continuously. At pitch ±90 the yaw and roll axes become the SAME axis, only their sum is determined, and feeding that back in as the next step\'s starting point means the ball stops turning where it was pushed and starts turning where the decomposition guessed. Measured: four degrees of real motion through the pole, and the printed numbers jump by 180 on two axes — both readings describing the same rotation. The ambiguity is confined to what is PRINTED now; the ball itself never stops.',
      'AND THE BALL ROLLS WITHOUT LIMIT. An arcball cannot exceed the sphere it is projected on — even measured step by step the point the hand maps to slides toward the equator and stops, so a straight drag ran out of SPHERE before it ran out of desk and sat at about a half turn while the hand kept going. A move is a roll now: the axis is perpendicular to the hand\'s travel across the screen, the angle is how far it travelled. One ball-radius is a quarter turn, which is what the arcball gave across the middle of the disc, so it feels the same where the arcball was honest and keeps going where it was not. Verified over 1800 accumulated turns — 5.7 full rotations — still orthonormal to 9.7e-14.',
      'THE RINGS KEEP THEIR HALF-TURN CEILING, AND THAT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT. A ring\'s angle is the pointer\'s position about its axis and the visible half of a ring spans 180°, so lifting it means changing what a ring IS. Three models were tried and none shipped: a flat azimuth collapses to a line for any ring whose axis lies in the plane of the screen, a wheel-and-lever model swings its gain eleven to one along a single ring and goes dead at the ends, and taking the tangent from the pointer rather than from the ring makes it answer to directions it cannot turn in. The committed arcball is better than all three. The BALL was what needed to turn freely; a ring is a fine adjustment.',
      'THE 360° KNOB — `knob({ endless: true })`. An ordinary knob is a potentiometer: 270° of sweep, a stop at each end, a 90° dead zone at the bottom. Right for a quantity that HAS a least and a most. An angle has neither — −180 and +180 are the same place, so a stop between them is a wall across open ground and the ball beside it turns straight through there. The endless one is the encoder instead: full travel, no dead zone, the value wraps rather than clamps. A flag rather than a second factory, because everything else about it is a knob.',
