      'AND IT IS ONE FUNCTION FOR BOTH PAINT PATHS, WHICH IS THE CONSTRAINT THAT DECIDED IT. The body comes from the texture; the inside of every cut is painted in closed form. Those are two places, and a texture-only split gives a blue north half with GREY PLASTIC INSIDE its grooves and wells. Everything asks `bodyAt` now, so a cut through the north half is cut into north-coloured material, walls and floor alike.',
      'GLOSS, AND THE DARK IS WHAT PAYS FOR IT. The specular went the other way once — .30 down to .11 — on the argument that a tight bright highlight is a WET look. True of the plastic that was there: a mid grey, where a highlight has little headroom and arrives as a pale smear. A dark body is the opposite case, with a long way between the material and white, so the hot spot has somewhere to be and reads as polish. Gloss and darkness are the same decision, which is why they arrived in the same sentence. The cuts stay matte at a fifth — at this sheen a machined face would look lacquered.',
